In case you are in the neighborhood and would like to stop by ...

Talk at U of Minnesota Sept 26:

Quantifying What it Would Take to Change an Inference:

Toward a Pragmatic Sociology

Pragmatic public policy informed by evidence requires one to make inferences from data.  But statistical inferences may be challenged because of uncontrolled bias.  There may be bias due to uncontrolled confounding variables or non-random selection into a sample.  We will answer the question about what it would take to change an inference by formalizing the sources of bias and quantifying the discourse about causal inferences in terms of those sources.  For example, we will transform challenges such as “But the inference of an effect of kindergarten retention on achievement might not be valid because of pre-existing differences between promoted and retained students” to questions such as “How much bias must there have been due to uncontrolled pre-existing differences to make the inference invalid?”  We will also generate statements such as “An omitted variable would have to be correlated at rrr with retention and with achievement to invalidate the inference.” Calculations will be presented using an app: http://konfound-it.com as well as commands in STATA and R.  Ultimately the approach leads to pragmatic sociology, informing action by quantifying the conditions that would invalidate inferences supporting that action.

Kenneth A. Frank, Michigan State University

